#{[17592186290493 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-3)+5\\cdot 1+x\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 2x-4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186290547 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}2t & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & =(1-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & =1-2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3} & =1-2{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).$"] [17592186290552 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}2t & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-(1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{4}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2} & =2{\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}\\right)-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1 \\\\ y & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1).$"] [17592186290553 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}2t & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-(1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{4} & =2{\\left(-\\frac{x}{3}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1 \\\\ y & =4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).$"] [17592186290554 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}2t & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & =(1-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & =1-2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2{\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}\\right) \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18} \\\\ y & =-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.$"] [17592186292813 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-((-4)\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{j}+((-4)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+8\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,-4,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292814 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-((-2)\\cdot (-2)-(-4)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+((-2)\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}-8\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-4,-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{2}=\\sqrt{21}.\n$$"] [17592186292815 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k})-(-2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(-2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-3,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186292816 "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})-(-2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j})-(-2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}-6\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{44} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{2}=\\sqrt{11}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\sqrt{11} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{11} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{22}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292825 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292826 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{13}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292827 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{d}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{d}_{x}^{2}+{d}_{y}^{2}+{d}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292828 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{d}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{d}_{x}^{2}+{d}_{y}^{2}+{d}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{c}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{d} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}+{c}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}-{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{c}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{d} & =\\frac{5}{3}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292829 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot x+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-2+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ -2+2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292830 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot x+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 2x+8 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-8 \\\\ x & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292832 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-3)-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-(6\\cdot (-3)-0\\cdot 5)\\mathbf{j}+(6\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot 5)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(6-0)\\mathbf{i}-(-18-0)\\mathbf{j}+(0-(-10))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6\\mathbf{i}+18\\mathbf{j}+10\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨6,18,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292833 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2x & 3 & 5 \\\\ 2x & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2x & 5 \\\\ 2x & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2x & 3 \\\\ 2x & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3-0)\\mathbf{i}-(-2x-10x)\\mathbf{j}+(0-6x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,12x,-6x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(12x)^{2}+(-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+144{x}^{2}+36{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{180{x}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{909} \\\\ \\sqrt{180{x}^{2}+9} & =\\sqrt{909} \\\\ 180{x}^{2}+9 & =909 \\\\ 180{x}^{2} & =900 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\sqrt{5},$ $x=\\sqrt{5}.$"] [17592186292837 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{6} & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=36.$"] [17592186292839 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨-\\sqrt{3},0,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\sqrt{3},10,-10⟩ \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{3})\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3})+0\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot (-10) \\\\  & =3+0+20 \\\\  & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292840 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨1,2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4+2+6 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292841 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & k & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & k \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(k\\cdot 4-(-2)\\cdot 2)-((-1)\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 2)-3((-1)\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot k) \\\\  & =-8k-8+10-6+9k \\\\  & =k-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ k-4 & =0 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=4.$"] [17592186292915 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -3 & \\le 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 3 \\\\ -3-2 & \\le 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\le 3-2 \\\\ -5 & \\le x\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-5\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 5 & \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-5\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-5 \\\\ -5 & \\le -5\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 5 \\\\ -5 & \\le y\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-5\\le y\\le 5.$"] [17592186295036 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(5\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{j}+(5\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(8+3)\\mathbf{i}-(20-6)\\mathbf{j}+(5+4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =11\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-14\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+9\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295037 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\mathbf{i}-(x-2)\\mathbf{j}+(0-4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,2-x,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(2-x)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4-4x+{x}^{2}+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+24} & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+24 & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=2.$"] [17592186295109 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}B,$$\n\nwhere $∠B$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $b.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=4, \\\\ a & =BC=8, \\\\ m∠B & ={40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{40}^{∘}=0.642\\,788,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{A} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot 4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘} \\\\  & =16\\cdot (0.642\\hspace{0.167em}788) \\\\  & \\approx 10.3,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to the **nearest tenth**."] [17592186295110 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A,$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}с & =AB=18, \\\\ b & =AC=12, \\\\ m∠A & ={120}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘}=0.866\\,025,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{A} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot 18\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{120}^{∘} \\\\  & =108\\cdot (0.866\\hspace{0.167em}025…) \\\\  & \\approx 93.5\\hspace{0.167em}{\\text{ft}}^{2},\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to the **nearest tenth**."] [17592186295111 "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACB$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/b1/b1d0cf7c5a7c6ad7f0f52c8686239920fabcb05be57372ae0c7c9c9491838a52.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACB,$ we find\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ACB} & =\\frac{1}{2}(10)(6)\\operatorname{sin}{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{60}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 25.981,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} & =2{\\mathcal{A}}_{ACB} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 25.981 \\\\  & =51.962.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, to the **nearest integer**, the area of the parallelogram is $52.$"] [17592186295123 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}B,$$\n\nwhere $∠B$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=5, \\\\ a & =BC=5, \\\\ m∠B & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}=0.866\\,025,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{A} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =12.5\\cdot (0.866\\hspace{0.167em}025) \\\\  & \\approx 10.83,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to **two decimal places**."] [17592186295125 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=10\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC, \\\\ m∠A & ={120}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{25\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 10\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{25\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 10\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{25\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =5b\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 25\\cancel{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} & =5b\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\ 5b & =25 \\\\ b & =5\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=5\\,\\text{ cm}.$"] [17592186295126 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{ mm}, \\\\ b & =AC=4\\,\\text{mm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $m∠A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{A} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ 3 & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}A & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}A & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295127 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,4,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(8-1)+2(-4-1)+3(-2-4) \\\\  & =14-10-18 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-14|=14.\n$$"] [17592186295128 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 1)-2((-3)\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2)+3((-3)\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =3(3+1)-2(-3+2)+3(-3-6) \\\\  & =12+2-27 \\\\  & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-13|=13.\n$$"] [17592186295164 "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}\\cdot (\\mathbf{y}\\times \\mathbf{z}) 